{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: Add an unknown-route fallback as part of the routing foundation**

The app routes only through the `routes` map — there is no `onGenerateRoute`/`onUnknownRoute`. Any navigation to an undefined name (a `pushNamed('/setings')` typo, a future deep link, or a platform intent) throws `onGenerateRoute returned null` and shows the grey/red error screen instead of a graceful fallback.

Since this PR establishes the initial routing system, wiring a fallback now is cheap and self-documenting:

```dart
MaterialApp(
  routes: {
    AppRoutes.start: (context) => const StartPage(),
    AppRoutes.shell: (context) => const ShellPage(),
  },
  onUnknownRoute: (settings) => MaterialPageRoute<dynamic>(
    builder: (context) => const StartPage(), // or a dedicated NotFoundPage
  ),
)
```

Optional at this stage — all current navigation goes through `AppRoutes` constants.
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment3.md"}

{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: Encode the test-only contract with `@visibleForTesting`**

The doc comment says `initialRoute` is *"used by tests"*. The idiomatic way to make that contract machine-checked is the annotation (available from `package:flutter/foundation.dart`, already a transitive dep):

```dart
import 'package:flutter/foundation.dart';
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';

class DarkCatsFactoryApp extends StatelessWidget {
  const DarkCatsFactoryApp({super.key, this.initialRoute});

  /// Overrides the route the app opens with; used by tests.
  @visibleForTesting
  final String? initialRoute;
}
```

This turns any future production use into an analyzer warning instead of relying on the comment staying true.
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment4.md"}

{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: Move the `capitalize` extension out of the page widget file**

The private, unnamed `extension on String` at the bottom of `shell_page.dart` is a general-purpose string utility living inside a widget file. It is invisible outside this file, so the first other caller will copy-paste it, and being unnamed it silently applies to every `String` literal in this file.

Options:

```dart
// lib/common/string_extensions.dart
extension StringCapitalization on String {
  String get capitalize =>
      isEmpty ? this : '${this[0].toUpperCase()}${substring(1)}';
}
```

Fine to defer until a second caller appears — but then move it, don't duplicate it. (Alternatively use `label: switch (destination) { ShellDestination.home => 'Home', ... }` and the extension disappears entirely.)
","path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment5.md"}

{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: Scope the destination finder instead of relying on `.last`**

`tapDestination` taps `find.text(label).last`, which works only because widget-tree order happens to put the `NavigationBar` label last relative to the (offstage) page content. Every placeholder page already renders the same strings as the nav labels (`Text('Home')`, `Text('Explore')`, …), so any future page that shows its label text visibly changes the match set.

Scoping the finder like the "shell offers the four destinations" test already does is explicit and order-independent:

```dart
Future<void> tapDestination(WidgetTester tester, String label) async {
  await tester.tap(
    find.descendant(of: find.byType(NavigationBar), matching: find.text(label)),
  );
  await tester.pumpAndSettle();
}
```

Also add a brief comment for why the NavigationBar scope is required.
